libjuice 3c40a354: on macOS and iOS each IPv6 /64 offers the address the system sends from

MPL-2.0, like src/udp.c; original file notice preserved unchanged.
2026-10-24: J.J. Boyd (KG4VCF), with AI-assisted implementation via Anthropic Claude Code.

udp_get_addrs offers one host candidate per IPv6 /64: has_duplicate_addr
compares the first 64 bits, and the comment above the gathering loop hopes
getifaddrs lists the preferred address first. On macOS and iOS it often
lists the secured (stable) address first, while the system sends from the
temporary one (net.inet6.ip6.prefer_tempaddr is 1), because the agent's
socket is bound to the wildcard address. Seen 2026-10-24 on a Mac with
2605:...:9ffc (secured) and 2605:...:61f2 (temporary): the Mac offered 9ffc
and sent every check from 61f2. Behind stateful IPv6 firewalls at both ends
neither side's checks matched a pinhole the other had opened, the IPv6 pair
never succeeded, and ICE settled on IPv4. A UDP probe between the same two
hosts passed in both directions whenever the offered and sending addresses
were the same.

On __APPLE__ only, for each IPv6 address getifaddrs lists, a UDP socket is
connected to another host in the same /64 (the address with its interface
identifier inverted, port 9; nothing is sent) and getsockname gives the
source the system picks. When that is another listed, usable address of the
same /64, this address is skipped and the /64 is offered with the address
the system sends from. In every other case the address is kept, so behavior
is unchanged: no socket, no route (connect fails), a source outside the /64,
the source is this address, or the source is not listed. IPv4 and Windows
are unchanged; Linux (which sends from the temporary address only with
use_tempaddr=2) is unchanged. libjuice upstream master (cb523763, checked
2026-10-24) has the same has_duplicate_addr and gathering loop.

